TwoDrive standard Debian build
=============================

This builds the stable GNOME OneDrive client, not the Flutter Development UI.
Install debhelper 13, dh-python, cargo/rustc >= 1.88, pkgconf, libsqlite3-dev,
python3, python3-gi and gir1.2-gtk-4.0, plus build-essential, lintian and xz-utils.
Use an OS/toolchain suitable for the intended runtime distribution.

From a clean committed Git tree, run:
    bash scripts/build-deb.sh -j2

The wrapper first prepares an immutable source tree and cargo-vendored locked
sources (this preparation may download dependencies). It then invokes:
    dpkg-buildpackage --build=full --no-sign
All package compilation/tests run offline with a fresh in-tree Cargo home.
Outputs, including the quilt .dsc, orig/component tarballs, binary/debug packages,
.buildinfo and .changes, are copied to dist/debian and checked for completeness.

To rebuild a downloaded source package, install the declared Build-Depends, put
all files referenced by the .dsc in one directory, then run:
    dpkg-source -x twodrive_0.2.14-2.dsc
    cd twodrive-0.2.14
    dpkg-buildpackage --build=binary --no-sign -j2
No cargo fetch or developer dependency cache is required for this rebuild.
Do not replace an already published original tarball with different contents
under the same upstream version.

The system SQLite is used with generated shared-library version dependencies.
Installation does not enable/start user services or modify user home directories.
The prior hand-built changelog is preserved in doc/legacy-debian-changelog.txt.

Ubuntu's native rustc may be older than the required compiler. Upstream CI uses a
verified rustup toolchain with dpkg-buildpackage -d in that environment only;
this bypass is explicitly recorded, and all other native dependencies are
installed beforehand. Normal distribution builds must satisfy Build-Depends
without -d. These upstream packages are not an upload to the Debian archive.
The Rust dependency and copyright audit, Debian-packaged dependency closure,
sponsorship and official distribution validation remain separate work.

The mandatory release policy is documented in doc/debian-packaging.md and applies
to both main and dev. Do not restore the manual dpkg-deb packaging path.
